Client Background:

You are a Cloud Engineer Consultant working with SwiftSupport, a growing tech startup that has developed an AI-powered ticket handling system.

They've successfully deployed their solution on AWS, but their initial focus was on functionality rather than security best practices. 

The system is now handling customer support tickets for several clients, making security a priority. Currently, the team of 12 employees shares AWS credentials, and there's minimal access control in place.

Current Setup:

Shared AWS credentials across the team

No proper IAM structure or role-based access

Minimal security configurations for services

No standardized deployment process

Current Infrastructure:

Single Page Application hosted via CloudFront and S3

WAF for security protection

API Gateway for request handling

Multiple Lambda functions for different operations

DynamoDB for ticket storage

Bedrock for AI processing

CloudWatch for monitoring

Team Structure & Access Needs:

5 Developers (need Lambda, API Gateway, and DynamoDB access)

2 DevOps Engineers (need full infrastructure access)

1 Security Specialist (needs WAF, IAM, and CloudWatch access)

2 Data Scientists (need Bedrock and read-only DynamoDB access)

2 Product Managers (need read-only access to monitoring and cost data)

--------- Your Task ---------

1 - Document the Current Architecture

Create a detailed architectural diagram documenting the current setup

Identify security gaps in the current implementation

2 - Secure AWS Account Access

Implement AWS Organizations

Secure the root account

Establish credential management best practices

3 - Implement IAM Best Practices

Create appropriate IAM users, groups, and roles

Developer group & users

DevOps group & users

Security group & user

Data Science group & users

Product Management group & users

4 - Enhance Service Security

Implement proper security for each service in the architecture:

Lambda function permissions

API Gateway authorization

DynamoDB access controls

WAF rule optimization

CloudFront security settings

5 - Implement These Permissions

Developers:

Lambda function development and deployment

API Gateway configuration

Limited DynamoDB table access

CloudWatch logs viewing

DevOps:

Full infrastructure management

CI/CD pipeline access

CloudWatch full access

Resource provisioning capabilities

Security Specialist:

WAF configuration

IAM administration

CloudWatch Logs and Alarms

Security audit capabilities

Data Scientists:

Full Bedrock access

Read-only DynamoDB access

Lambda function access for AI components

Product Managers:

CloudWatch dashboard access

Cost Explorer and Budgets

Read-only resource visibility

--------- What to Submit ---------

Documentation and Screenshots

Comprehensive documentation explaining:

The current architecture and its security vulnerabilities

Your implementation approach and security improvements

Reasoning behind permission structures

In the documentation include screenshots showing:

IAM groups, users, and roles structure

Security configurations for key services (WAF, API Gateway, Lambda)

Permission boundaries and policies for each team

CloudWatch monitoring and alerting setup

How service-to-service authentication is secured